So LinkedHashMap has a constructor parameter `accessOrder` — when set to true, it reorders entries by access time (most recently accessed moves to end) instead of insertion order.

You can use this to build an LRU cache by subclassing and overriding `removeEldestEntry()`:

```java
class LRUCache<K,V> extends LinkedHashMap<K,V> {
    private final int capacity;
    LRUCache(int capacity) {
        super(capacity, 0.75f, true); // access-order=true
        this.capacity = capacity;
    }
    @Override
    protected boolean removeEldestEntry(Map.Entry<K,V> eldest) {
        return size() > capacity; // remove LRU when full
    }
}
```

This is a well-known interview question (LeetCode #146). The LinkedHashMap solution is elegant but single-threaded. For concurrent use, add synchronization or use a different approach.
